{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2393 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "28f6a614743e4acdf1374e8adede42137756caa3309804b7cf421500d2cef22b" :edb/db-before-t 2392 :edb/db-after-t 2393 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535705 50 #inst "2026-10-09T05:36:53.705Z" 13194139535705 true] [17592186164328 1104 #edb/ref 17592186164331 13194139535705 true] [17592186164476 1104 #edb/ref 17592186164478 13194139535705 true] [17592186164479 1104 #edb/ref 17592186164481 13194139535705 true] [17592186166477 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-8x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-8y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSketch the phase portrait of the linear system above." 13194139535705 true] [17592186166477 1100 "${x^{′}(t) =-8x(t); y^{′}(t) =-8y(t)$\nSketch the phase portrait of the linear system above." 13194139535705 false] [17592186166477 1145 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/01d652156896744699e5533725f537e9f203031fab369accc10c912839ebad11.png)\nwhere ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/2dff913b6a0163c9dff6e28d81690472f043de69e4796dffb58b16b933165450.png)\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=-8$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/c3fe70f694806cdfa2bb64064e3e97e8142c05c249def67cd22e5c21244a233b.png)\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/3fd44b389c505756db88609e4ed7b78cdc6a2edeeb087edf3ed20034a9c909cf.png)\n\nNotice that for each vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/fe0e0ebbdd9f7cd29ca5d2ca7633f829e682057024ba6c783fe7e10106a0bbfd.png) we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/0a6ee4e5731289a20e011783360e0b17aa8e425995e13ee34950efd31cf73922.png)\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/a30588ff9f9c3f7db4b7840afddf97a38df27ef7cdef48f4a3037e76836cb6a1.png)\n\nSince $e^{-8t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty,$ so the origin is a stable node.\n\nThe phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/4c337f7f6b80f340859844e8217525db98230660d22e760c70218f0c282d21e9.png)" 13194139535705 true] [17592186166477 1145 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=-8\n$$\n\nwith the corresponding eigenvectors $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNotice that for each vector $\\begin{bmatrix}c_{1} \\\\ c_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}c_{1} \\\\ c_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}c_{1} \\\\ c_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSince $e^{-8t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty$, these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty$, so the origin is a stable node.\n\nThe phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3240/Source/Images/24987f26ca917591ec4d8c2ddbbf8735.png)" 13194139535705 false] [17592186166478 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector $\\mathbf{v} = [1, \\ -1]^T$ corresponds to the repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=-2$ of the system's matrix $A.$ The vector $\\mathbf{w} = [0, \\ 1]^T$ is such that $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$ Sketch the phase portrait of the linear system above." 13194139535705 true] [17592186166478 1100 "${x^{′}(t) =-x(t) + y(t); y^{′}(t) =-x(t) - 3y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector $\\mathbf{v} = [1, \\ -1]^T$ corresponds to the repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=-2$ of the system's matrix $A$. The vector $\\mathbf{w} = [0, \\ 1]^T$ is such that $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}$. Sketch the phase portrait of the linear system above." 13194139535705 false] [17592186166478 1145 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=-2$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}$, the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t} + c_2 (\\mathbf{v} te^{\\lambda t} + \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\,)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} &= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}e^{-2t} + c_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}te^{-2t} + \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{-2t}) \\\\\n&= c_{1}e^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}e^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ -t + 1\\end{bmatrix}, c_{1}, c_{2} ∈ R\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation\n$y= -x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line.\n\nSince $e^{-2t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty$, these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty$.\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3240/Source/Images/40daf39b4da4ba08b07408a1a52d58e0.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ -t + 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nWhen $c_2=1$, we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n{x = te^{-2t}; y = (-t + 1)e^{-2t}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ | $-1.36$ | $0$ | $0.18$ |\n| $y$ | $4.08$ | $1$ | $0.18$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3240/Source/Images/93a4716b66152e1696615a978d123f9f.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3240/Source/Images/c14ad78173ebf05ed8f6540484bba45a.png)" 13194139535705 false] [17592186166478 1145 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=-2$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $\\mathbf{v},$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}  + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\, ),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nIn our case, \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/15e5c3f06172ad8218375d20178b96a80de122300761fa4a615aca099d13c52a.png) \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ -t+1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/daa87299ff267ce47cf44b06b218f533de30e780b12905d0e0a1888a50893dd0.png) has the equation \n\n$$\ny= -x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/869e6bf67c0a5826b8d29130e8bf2107674508a103c4fb5a3d7d70ab78577090.png)\nlie on that line. \n\nSince $e^{-2t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/523dccf73169d02b6bdf4297e96ec84bcc6c43213cdd722000f3a987a534b218.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/4fc6300682b6e28d47e43802aba9427e29c606fc6c4f77edad874e7c0e4e2c01.png)\nWhen $c_2=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=t{e}^{-2t} \\\\ y=(-t+1){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-1.36$ | $0$ | $0.18$ |\n| $y$ | $4.08$ | $1$ | $0.18$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/dbf5a13ecd5e81c399204fd29d235e410fcb62274555cf3e5b32a383c1078d24.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/6c84cd04f3b037bee48d02d3183114e2668fbbd110bd44e9311e7ff454db8bac.png)" 13194139535705 true] [17592186166479 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=6x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=5$ of multiplicity two. Determine the type and stability of the equilibrium point for this linear system." 13194139535705 true] [17592186166479 1100 "${x^{′}(t) = 6x(t) + y(t); y^{′}(t) =-x(t) + 4y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=5$ of multiplicity two. Determine the type and stability of the equilibrium point for this linear system." 13194139535705 false] [17592186166479 1145 "The matrix of our system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/7d16f04f098fc32370423b1b7adaa84c14b92291bde82672b65977b0498ec011.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is positive, we have a unstable node.\n\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a degenerate node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/32f9fd5ff34d75a7b82427a5ab7233613da3bdbaefdaef37058e65b1691b6c4a.png)" 13194139535705 true] [17592186166479 1145 "The matrix of our system is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is positive, we have a unstable node.\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a degenerate node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3240/Source/Images/3f407fe5a2055332f131e6ddc696a3a8.png)" 13194139535705 false] [17592186166851 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362231 13194139535705 true] [17592186166851 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362238 13194139535705 true] [17592186166851 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362245 13194139535705 true] [17592186166851 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362252 13194139535705 true] [17592186166851 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362259 13194139535705 true] [17592186166852 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362266 13194139535705 true] [17592186166853 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362303 13194139535705 true] [17592186166853 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362310 13194139535705 true] [17592186166853 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362317 13194139535705 true] [17592186166853 1020 #edb/ref 17592186362324 13194139535705 true] [17592186167156 1017 #edb/ref 17592186164323 13194139535705 false] [17592186167156 1017 #edb/ref 17592186362198 13194139535705 true] [17592186167156 1017 #edb/ref 17592186362203 13194139535705 true] [17592186167156 1017 #edb/ref 17592186362208 13194139535705 true] [17592186167156 1017 #edb/ref 17592186362213 13194139535705 true] [17592186167156 1017 #edb/ref 17592186362216 13194139535705 true] [17592186167156 1017 #edb/ref 17592186362221 13194139535705 true] [17592186167156 1017 #edb/ref 17592186362226 13194139535705 true] [17592186167156 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535705 true] [17592186167156 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)+5y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector $v=[2,\\text{ }2]^{T}$ corresponds to the repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of the system's matrix $A.$ The vector $w=[-1,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{3t}+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{{field-1}}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}{{field-2}}+{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}2t-1 \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{3t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/c993f6fc9eb12c0d8f60c420519a7db4a5cf78da9f0c24d0ade87b0a96590f59.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}2t-1 \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=4,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=4(2t-1){e}^{3t} \\\\ y={{field-6}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | {{field-7}} | $-4$ | $-4.37$ |\n| $y$ | {{field-8}} | $0$ | $2.92$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/6cc714f7f703eaebf31983882cbe988742ea47a9e280c7fbee1f44c877952b63.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/26ab713a8961e5668ee3663e0e5ac40ffbd6ea1dd02416ede0e2cbaaad4b63bf.png)" 13194139535705 true] [17592186167156 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\n${x^{′}(t) = x(t) + 2y(t); y^{′}(t) =-2x(t) + 5y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector $v = [2, 2]^{T}$ corresponds to the repeated eigenvalue $λ = 3$ of the system's matrix $A$. The vector $w = [- 1, 0]^{T}$ is such that $(A - λI)w = v$. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n$e^{-t}$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}" 13194139535705 false] [17592186167156 1145 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =3,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{3t}+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}2t-1 \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/c993f6fc9eb12c0d8f60c420519a7db4a5cf78da9f0c24d0ade87b0a96590f59.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}2t-1 \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=4,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=4(2t-1){e}^{3t} \\\\ y=8t{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-1.79$ | $-4$ | $-4.37$ |\n| $y$ | $-0.89$ | $0$ | $2.92$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/6cc714f7f703eaebf31983882cbe988742ea47a9e280c7fbee1f44c877952b63.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069697/assets/26ab713a8961e5668ee3663e0e5ac40ffbd6ea1dd02416ede0e2cbaaad4b63bf.png)" 13194139535705 true] [17592186167178 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535705 true] [17592186167178 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode." 13194139535705 true] [17592186167178 1100 "${x^{′}(t) = x(t); y^{′}(t) = y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Select the correct options below regarding the given system. The equilibrium point (origin) is a\n{{field-1}} {{field-2}}\nnode." 13194139535705 false] [17592186167178 1145 "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is $\\lambda =1$ and it's positive, we have a $\\text{unstable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there are infinitely many linearly independent eigenvectors. So, we have a $\\text{star}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows